-- title:
--   An orientation bit matching strict places to branch components
-- statement:
--   The data are: a prime $p$ with $5 \le p$; a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` satisfying `A.LiesOverPrime p`, i.e. $p$ lies in the nonunits of $A$; and a Deligne–Rapoport model package $\mathfrak X$ for $p$ (`DRModelPackage p`), which bundles the two-chart integral model `DRModel p` of the modular function field at level $p$ together with its properness, flatness, integrality and normality, a curve model $M_0$ of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldFull}\ p$ over $\mathbb Q$ and a curve model $\mathfrak X.\mathtt{M\eta}$ of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\ p$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with isomorphisms $e_0$, $e_\eta$ onto the corresponding base changes of `DRModel p`, the compatibility of $e_\eta$ with the arithmetic Galois action on places, the two sections $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{inf}}$, $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{zero}}$, the smooth locus, and the further components of the structure, among them the morphisms $\mathfrak X.\mathtt{compZero}$ and $\mathfrak X.\mathtt{compInf}$ used in the conclusion.
--
--   First group (reading of the generic fibre). The open subscheme of $\mathfrak X.\mathtt{M\eta}.C$ obtained as the preimage, under $e_\eta$ followed by the first projection of the pullback of `DRModel.toBase p` along $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\mathbb Z \to \overline{\mathbb Q}$, of the image of the total space of the finite chart $\iota_{\mathrm{Fin}}$ of the two-chart model, is assumed nonempty ($\mathtt{hne\eta}$). The hypothesis $\mathtt{hM\eta}$ pins down the reading of the function field on that open set: for every element $a$ of the finite chart algebra $\mathrm{chartAlgFin}\ \mathbb Z\ (\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldFull}\ p)\ (\mathrm{jFull}\ p)$, the element of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\ p$ obtained by transporting $a$ through the chart's global-sections isomorphism, pulling it back along $e_\eta$ followed by the first projection, taking its germ in the function field of $\mathfrak X.\mathtt{M\eta}.C$ and applying $\mathfrak X.\mathtt{M\eta}.\mathtt{ffEquiv}^{-1}$, has, as a Laurent series over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, the value $\mathrm{coeffEmb}$ of the Laurent series of $a$ over $\mathbb Q$; that is, chart functions are read as their $q$-expansions base changed to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$.
--
--   Second group (level transport on places). A bijection $e_{\mathrm{Pl}}$ between the places of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\ (1\cdot p)$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ and those of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\ p$ is given, together with $\mathtt{hePl\_fun}$: whenever $f$ and $f'$ in the two fields have the same underlying Laurent series, $f$ lies in the valuation subring of $V$ if and only if $f'$ lies in that of $e_{\mathrm{Pl}}V$, and the residual evaluations agree, $V.\mathtt{evalAt}\,f = (e_{\mathrm{Pl}}V).\mathtt{evalAt}\,f'$.
--
--   Third group (specialisation at $A$). A field $k$ of characteristic $p$, perfect and algebraically closed, a ring homomorphism $\mathrm{red} : A \to k$, modular polynomial data $\mathrm{data}$ for $p$ with the Kronecker congruence $h_{\mathrm{Kr}}$ (the bivariate reduction of $\Phi$ modulo $p$ equals $(X'^p - X)(X' - X^p)$), integrality hypotheses $h_\alpha$, $h_\beta$ for the Hecke $\bar\alpha$, $\bar\beta$ maps at level $1$ and prime $p$, and a place specialisation $P : \mathrm{PlaceSpecialization}\ A\ p\ 1\ \mathrm{data}\ h_{\mathrm{Kr}}\ k\ \mathrm{red}\ h_\alpha\ h_\beta$, together with a prolongation tuple $R$ for $P$ (a pair of regular prolongations of $A$ to $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\ (1\cdot p)$ with residue field $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldFullC}$ of the residue field of $A$, linked by the Atkin–Lehner involution and compatible with reduction of coefficients).
--
--   Fourth group (the inertia discrete valuation ring). A discrete valuation ring $O$ which is a domain, an isomorphism $e_O$ of $O$ with the pullback of $A$ along the inclusion of the inertia-fixed field $\mathrm{fixedField}(A.\mathtt{inertiaSubgroupIn}\ \mathbb Q)$ into $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, the condition $\mathtt{h\varpi O}$ that the maximal ideal of $O$ is generated by $p$, a fraction field $K$ of $O$, a ring homomorphism $\iota_K : K \to \overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $\mathtt{h\iota K}$ saying that on elements of $O$ it agrees with the embedding determined by $e_O$, with $\mathtt{h\iota A}$ saying that $\iota_K$ carries $O$ into $A$, and a homomorphism $\mathrm{to\kappa} : O \to k$ with $\mathtt{hto\kappa}$ saying that $\mathrm{to\kappa}$ is $\mathrm{red}$ applied to the image of $O$ in $A$ under the same embedding.
--
--   Fifth group (reading of the function field of the $O$-model). The pullback $\mathcal X_O$ of `DRModel.toBase p` along $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\mathbb Z \to O$ is assumed integral ($\mathtt{hint}$), and a ring homomorphism $\varphi$ from the function field of $\mathcal X_O$ to $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\ (1\cdot p)$ is given with: $\mathtt{h\varphi O}$, that $\varphi$ sends the germ at the generic point of the global section of $\mathcal X_O$ coming from $a \in O$ via the second projection to the image of $\iota_K(a)$ under $\overline{\mathbb Q} \to \mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\ (1\cdot p)$; the nonemptiness ($\mathtt{hne}$) of the preimage in $\mathcal X_O$ of the finite chart open; and $\mathtt{h\varphi j}$, that for every $a$ in the finite chart algebra, $\varphi$ of the germ in the function field of $\mathcal X_O$ of the pulled-back chart function $a$ is, as a Laurent series over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, the $\mathrm{coeffEmb}$ image of the Laurent series of $a$ over $\mathbb Q$.
--
--   The conclusion asserts the existence of one Boolean $\mathrm{swap}$ such that the following holds for every place $V$ of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\ (1\cdot p)$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ which is fixed by the arithmetic Galois action of every $\sigma$ in $A.\mathtt{inertiaSubgroupIn}\ \mathbb Q$, i.e. $\mathrm{arithmeticGalois}(\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldFull}\ (1\cdot p))\,\sigma \cdot V = V$, and for every morphism $s : \operatorname{Spec} O \to \mathcal X_O$ which is a section of the second projection ($s$ followed by $\mathtt{pullback.snd}$ is the identity) and which passes through the point of $V$, in the precise sense that the morphism $\operatorname{Spec}\overline{\mathbb Q} \to \operatorname{Spec} O$ induced by the embedding $O \cong A \cap \mathrm{fixedField}(A.\mathtt{inertiaSubgroupIn}\ \mathbb Q) \hookrightarrow \overline{\mathbb Q}$, followed by $s$ and by the first projection, equals the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-point $(\mathfrak X.\mathtt{M\eta}.\mathtt{pointEquivPlace})^{-1}(e_{\mathrm{Pl}}V)$ followed by $e_\eta$ and by the first projection:
--
--   (i) if $P.\mathtt{IsStrictFst}\,V$ holds, that is $\mathrm{frobOnPlacesGeomLevel}\,k\,1\,\mathrm{data}\,h_{\mathrm{Kr}}(P.\mathtt{reduceFst}\,V) = P.\mathtt{reduceSnd}\,V$ while the twofold Frobenius image of $P.\mathtt{reduceFst}\,V$ differs from $P.\mathtt{reduceFst}\,V$, then the image $s(\mathfrak m_O)$ of the closed point of $O$ under the underlying map of $s$ lies in the range of the underlying map of $\mathfrak X.\mathtt{compZero}\,k$ followed by $\mathrm{DRModel.baseChangeMap}\ \mathrm{to\kappa}$ if $\mathrm{swap}$ is true and of $\mathfrak X.\mathtt{compInf}\,k$ followed by that base change map otherwise, and does not lie in the range of the underlying map of the other of the two composites;
--
--   (ii) if $P.\mathtt{IsStrictSnd}\,V$ holds, that is $P.\mathtt{reduceFst}\,V$ equals the Frobenius image of $P.\mathtt{reduceSnd}\,V$ while the twofold Frobenius image of $P.\mathtt{reduceSnd}\,V$ differs from $P.\mathtt{reduceSnd}\,V$, then $s(\mathfrak m_O)$ lies in the range of $\mathfrak X.\mathtt{compInf}\,k$ followed by $\mathrm{DRModel.baseChangeMap}\ \mathrm{to\kappa}$ if $\mathrm{swap}$ is true and of $\mathfrak X.\mathtt{compZero}\,k$ followed by that map otherwise, and does not lie in the range of the other composite.
--
--   Thus the same bit $\mathrm{swap}$ serves for all inertia-fixed places $V$ and all sections $s$ through the corresponding point: strict-first places specialise into one of the two branch components of the geometric special fibre and strict-second places into the other, and in each case the point avoids the complementary component.
--
--   This is the branch-matching step for the Deligne–Rapoport model of the modular curve of level $p$: the two components of the mod $p$ fibre are oriented consistently against the two kinds of strict places produced by the Kronecker congruence, so that the Frobenius bookkeeping on places can be transferred to the components through which integral sections pass. It is used by [`ModularCurve.DRResolvedModelPackage.exists_nodeEquiv_swap_forall_comp_eq_dict_of_sections_of_charts`](thm.html#ModularCurve.DRResolvedModelPackage.exists_nodeEquiv_swap_forall_comp_eq_dict_of_sections_of_charts) in the assembly of the resolved-model package.
